  "worked_solution": "Notice that $f(x,y)$ equals zero when $x=1$ and $y=2.$ These lines divide the $xy$-plane into four regions, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052876/assets/4244ec01c3ca7ea666967abfea565c5674f8740033f365b138a9488899b2017a.png)\n\nTo determine the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region, we pick four test points, one for each region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052876/assets/4d115bf92aa8255a17ee6e658026570c683d1bc65fc342463d7e66f58d4df554.png)\n\nNext, we calculate the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region using a sign table:\n\n|  | Test point | $(x-1)$ | $(2-y)$ | $f(x,y)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Top-Left | $(0,3)$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| Top-Right | $(2,3)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| Bottom-Left | $(0,1)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| Bottom-Right | $(2,1)$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n\nUsing the *last* column of the table above, we get the following picture:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052876/assets/91e3506692c75397376f3addbcab0636bb817462eec529206ba3a62c84d901be.png)\n\nFinally, we fill in the required sign table.\n\n| $y>2$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<2$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n|  | $x<1$ | $x>1$ |",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
